var e=[{topicId:`pj-dc-act`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940 with the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945 is the central law that controls the import, manufacture, distribution and sale of drugs and cosmetics in India. The Act gives the powers; the Rules give the working detail such as forms, licences, schedules and labelling.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`What the Act covers`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Chapter II - the technical advisory machinery (DTAB, Central Drugs Laboratory, DCC).`,`Chapter III - import of drugs and cosmetics.`,`Chapter IV - manufacture, sale and distribution of drugs and cosmetics.`,`Chapter IVA - Ayurvedic, Siddha and Unani drugs.`,`Chapter V - miscellaneous provisions, rule-making power, penalties.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The four words examiners love`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Term`,`Simple meaning`],rows:[[`Drug`,`Substance used for diagnosis, treatment, mitigation or prevention of disease in humans or animals; also devices and components notified as drugs`],[`Cosmetic`,`Article meant to be applied to the human body for cleansing, beautifying or altering appearance`],[`Misbranded`,`The label or claim is false, misleading or not as prescribed`],[`Adulterated`,`The drug itself is filthy, contaminated, decomposed or contains a harmful added substance`],[`Spurious`,`The drug pretends to be something it is not - wrong manufacturer, imitation, substituted drug`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Misbranded vs spurious`,text:`Misbranding is a labelling defect - the product is genuine but the label lies. Spurious is an identity fraud - the product pretends to be another manufacturer’s product or is an imitation. Adulteration is a quality defect in the substance itself.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Who enforces it`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Central Drugs Standard Control Organisation (CDSCO), headed by the Drugs Controller General (India), handles import, new drugs, clinical trials and certain notified classes.`,`State Licensing Authorities (State Drugs Controllers) grant manufacturing and sale licences and appoint drug inspectors.`,`Drug Inspectors take samples, inspect premises and launch prosecutions.`,`Government Analysts test the samples and issue a report; the Central Drugs Laboratory is the referee laboratory.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`One-liners that repeat`,text:`Act 1940, Rules 1945. Central Drugs Laboratory is at Kolkata. DTAB is the technical advisory body; DCC exists to secure uniformity of administration between the Centre and the States.`}]},{topicId:`pj-dc-act`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level you need the definitions with their section numbers, the composition and function of the two advisory bodies, the powers of inspectors and analysts, and the graded penalty structure. Most jurisprudence questions in GPAT come from exactly this list.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Key definitions and their sections`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Provision`,`Section`,`Core idea`],rows:[[`Drug`,`3(b)`,`Medicines, substances for diagnosis or treatment, notified devices, components of formulations`],[`Cosmetic`,`3(aaa)`,`Applied to the body for cleansing, beautifying, promoting attractiveness`],[`Ayurvedic, Siddha, Unani drug`,`3(a)`,`Manufactured per formulae in the authoritative books of the First Schedule`],[`Misbranded drug`,`17`,`False, misleading or non-conforming label or colouring`],[`Adulterated drug`,`17A`,`Filthy, putrid, decomposed, unsanitary conditions, harmful container, unsafe colour`],[`Spurious drug`,`17B`,`Imitation, substituted, falsely claims another manufacturer, label bears a fictitious maker`],[`Misbranded cosmetic`,`17C`,`Harmful colour, false label`],[`Spurious cosmetic`,`17D`,`Imitates or is a substitute for another cosmetic`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Advisory and testing machinery`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Body`,`Section`,`Role`,`Head`],rows:[[`DTAB`,`5`,`Advises Centre and States on technical matters; framing of rules`,`Director General of Health Services`],[`Central Drugs Laboratory`,`6`,`Referee testing, standards, statutory analysis`,`Director of CDL, Kolkata`],[`DCC`,`7`,`Secures uniformity of administration of the Act across India`,`Nominee of Central Government`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The DTAB has ex-officio, nominated and elected members, and the Drugs Controller General (India) acts as its Member-Secretary.`,`The DCC has representatives of the Central Government and of every State, which is what makes it the uniformity body.`,`Corresponding bodies exist for the Ayurveda side - the ASU Drugs Technical Advisory Board and the ASU Drugs Consultative Committee.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Inspectors and Government Analysts`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Government Analysts are appointed under Section 20 by the Central or State Government.`,`Inspectors are appointed under Section 21 by the Central or State Government, by notification.`,`Powers of Inspectors are in Section 22 - inspect premises, take samples, seize stock and records, stop conveyance.`,`Procedure of Inspectors is in Section 23 - sample divided into portions, receipt given, one portion to the person, one to the Government Analyst, one retained for the court.`,`Section 25 makes the Government Analyst’s report evidence, and allows the accused to challenge it by sending the court portion to the Central Drugs Laboratory, whose report is then conclusive.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Licensing pathway (Rules 1945)`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Activity`,`Application`,`Licence`],rows:[[`Import of drugs`,`Form 8`,`Form 10`],[`Manufacture (non-specified drugs)`,`Form 24`,`Form 25`],[`Manufacture (Schedule C, C1 and X)`,`Form 27`,`Form 28`],[`Retail or wholesale sale`,`Form 19`,`Form 20 / 21 (and 20B / 21B wholesale)`]],caption:`Schedule A prescribes the forms; Schedule C and C1 items are licensed with central approval.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Personnel rule that is asked`,text:`Retail sale of drugs must be made by or under the personal supervision of a registered pharmacist. For wholesale, a competent person is accepted - a graduate with about one year of experience in dealing in drugs, or a person with a pass in the school leaving examination and about four years of such experience.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Labelling essentials`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Proper name of the drug in a font at least as large as the brand name, and the name and address of the manufacturer.`,`Batch or lot number, manufacturing licence number, date of manufacture and date of expiry.`,`Net content, and the content of active ingredient in metric units.`,`Symbol Rx in red on the top left corner of the label for Schedule H, and NRx in red for Schedule X.`,`Schedule G drugs carry the caution that it is dangerous to take the preparation except under medical supervision.`,`Prices, storage conditions where required, and the statement for physician samples or for use in hospitals only where applicable.`]}]},{topicId:`pj-dc-act`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions test the penalty ladder, the special enforcement provisions added by amendment, and the exemptions that appear in Chapter III and Chapter IV. Penalty figures have been revised by amendment, so learn the structure - which offence is graver than which - rather than only the numbers.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The penalty ladder`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Offence class`,`Broad punishment`],rows:[[`Spurious or adulterated drug likely to cause death or grievous hurt`,`Imprisonment not less than ten years, extendable to imprisonment for life, plus a heavy fine`],[`Other adulterated or spurious drugs`,`Imprisonment not less than three years, extendable to five years, plus fine`],[`Drug not of standard quality (other than the above)`,`Imprisonment for a shorter term with fine; court may reduce in specified cases`],[`Manufacture or sale without a valid licence`,`Imprisonment with fine, graded by the class of drug involved`],[`Repeat offence`,`Enhanced imprisonment and fine`]],caption:`Section 27 for drugs, Section 27A for cosmetics, Section 28 series for records and obstruction offences.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Cognizable and non-bailable`,text:`The serious spurious and adulterated drug offences were made cognizable and non-bailable by the 2008 amendment, and Special Courts may be designated to try them. Do not assume every offence under the Act is bailable - the gravity of the drug offence decides.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Special central powers`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Section 10A and Section 26A allow prohibition of import, manufacture, sale or distribution of a drug or cosmetic in the public interest, and are the route used to ban fixed dose combinations judged irrational.`,`Section 33 gives the Central Government the power to make rules after consulting the DTAB.`,`Section 33P allows the Central Government to give directions to a State Government on execution of the Act.`,`Section 18 is the operative prohibition section - it forbids manufacture or sale of drugs that are not of standard quality, misbranded, adulterated or spurious, and forbids doing so without a licence.`,`Section 16 fixes the standards of quality by reference to the Second Schedule.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Import provisions and exemptions`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Section 10 prohibits import of drugs that are not of standard quality, misbranded, adulterated, spurious, or whose import is banned in the public interest.`,`Small quantities may be imported for personal use, and for the purpose of examination, test or analysis, subject to conditions.`,`Schedule D lists the classes of drugs and cosmetics exempted from specified provisions of Chapter III.`,`Registration of the foreign manufacturing site and the drug, in addition to the import licence, is required for import for sale.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Sampling arithmetic`,text:`When an inspector takes a sample for test, the quantity is divided into portions - one is given to the person from whom it is taken, one is sent to the Government Analyst and one is retained for production before the court. If the Analyst report is disputed, the court portion goes to the Central Drugs Laboratory and that report is conclusive evidence.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Boundary cases`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A device may be a drug only when notified as such; medical devices are now largely handled by the Medical Devices Rules made under the same Act.`,`Blood and blood components are treated as drugs and blood banks are licensed under the Rules.`,`Ayurvedic, Siddha and Unani drugs sit in Chapter IVA with their own misbranded, adulterated and spurious definitions and their own GMP schedule.`,`Cosmetics have no expiry-date requirement equivalent to drugs in every case, but they must carry batch number, manufacturing licence number and the list of ingredients as required by the Rules.`]}]},{topicId:`pj-schedules`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The Schedules are the appendices to the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945. Each one is a list - of forms, of fees, of drug classes, of standards or of requirements. GPAT almost always asks a direct recall question of the form "Schedule X deals with?".`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Master table of Schedules`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Schedule`,`Deals with`],rows:[[`A`,`Specimen forms of applications, licences and certificates`],[`B`,`Fees for test or analysis by the Central Drugs Laboratory and Government Analysts`],[`C`,`Biological and special products - sera, vaccines, toxins, antitoxins, insulin, hormones`],[`C1`,`Other special products requiring special treatment or storage`],[`D`,`Classes of drugs exempted from specified import provisions`],[`E1`,`List of poisonous substances under the Ayurvedic, Siddha and Unani systems`],[`F`,`Requirements for blood banks and blood products, and standards for certain sterile articles`],[`FF`,`Standards for ophthalmic preparations`],[`G`,`Drugs to be used only under medical supervision - caution statement required`],[`H`,`Prescription drugs - sale only on the prescription of a registered medical practitioner`],[`H1`,`Restricted prescription drugs with a separate register and a boxed warning`],[`J`,`Diseases a drug may not claim to prevent or cure`],[`K`,`Classes of drugs exempted from specified provisions of Chapter IV`],[`M`,`Good Manufacturing Practices and requirements of premises, plant and equipment`],[`N`,`Minimum equipment for the efficient running of a pharmacy`],[`O`,`Standards for disinfectant fluids`],[`P`,`Life period - shelf life and storage conditions of drugs`],[`Q`,`Colours, dyes and pigments permitted in cosmetics and soaps`],[`R`,`Standards for mechanical contraceptives and certain notified devices`],[`S`,`Standards for cosmetics as laid down by the Bureau of Indian Standards`],[`T`,`GMP for Ayurvedic, Siddha and Unani medicines`],[`U`,`Records of manufacture and of raw materials to be maintained`],[`V`,`Standards for patent and proprietary medicines`],[`X`,`Narcotic and psychotropic drugs needing a special licence and duplicate prescription`],[`Y`,`Requirements and guidelines for clinical trials and for new drug import or manufacture`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`For the letters that clash`,text:`M is Manufacturing practice, N is Necessary equipment for a pharmacy, P is Period of life, U is Upkeep of records, V is Vitamin and other limits in patent medicines, X is eXtra control (narcotics), Y is clinical trial studY.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`H versus X versus G`,text:`Schedule G is not a prescription schedule - it only needs a caution statement. Schedule H needs a prescription and the red Rx symbol. Schedule X needs a special licence, a prescription in duplicate and the red NRx symbol.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Some schedules have been omitted or replaced over the years - Schedule I and Schedule W are the classic examples - so a question asking "which schedule has been omitted" usually has one of those as the answer.`}]},{topicId:`pj-schedules`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A handful of schedules carry most of the marks because they govern day-to-day pharmacy practice. Learn C and C1, G, H, H1, X, M, N, P, U, V and Y in detail - the rest can stay at one-line recall.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Schedule C and C1 - biological and special products`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Schedule C: sera, vaccines, toxins, antitoxins, antigens, insulin, pituitary and other hormone preparations, bacteriophages, sterile injectable preparations of specified classes.`,`Schedule C1: other special products requiring special treatment during manufacture or storage, for example preparations of digitalis and ergot, certain hormone and steroid preparations and vitamins of biological origin.`,`Both classes are manufactured under Form 28 (application Form 27) and sold under Form 21 retail or Form 21B wholesale.`,`Manufacture of some Schedule C and C1 items, notably vaccines, sera and r-DNA products, requires the concurrence of the Central Licence Approving Authority.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Prescription and record schedules`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Schedule`,`Label mark`,`Prescription`,`Record retention`],rows:[[`G`,`Caution statement, no Rx symbol`,`Not statutorily required`,`Normal sale records`],[`H`,`Rx in red, top left corner`,`Prescription of an RMP required`,`Prescription record kept two years`],[`H1`,`Rx in red plus boxed warning with red border`,`Prescription required`,`Separate bound register kept three years`],[`X`,`NRx in red, top left corner`,`Prescription in duplicate`,`Prescription and register kept two years`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Schedule G examples: antineoplastic and cytotoxic agents, carbimazole, and insulin preparations - drugs where unsupervised use is hazardous.`,`Schedule H1 was created to slow antimicrobial resistance and misuse - it covers several third and fourth generation antibiotics, the anti-tuberculosis drugs and a few habit-forming agents such as tramadol.`,`Schedule X examples: barbiturates such as amobarbital, pentobarbital and secobarbital, plus amphetamine and methylphenidate; a separate Schedule X licence in Form 20F retail or Form 20G wholesale is needed.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Premises, records and standards schedules`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Schedule M sets GMP and the requirements of premises, plant and equipment; Part I is general and Part II gives specific requirements for particular dosage forms. Related schedules are M-I for homoeopathic medicines, M-II for cosmetics and M-III for medical devices.`,`Schedule N lists the minimum equipment for the efficient running of a pharmacy - balances, measures, mortars, water bath, refrigerator, and the reference books including the Indian Pharmacopoeia.`,`Schedule P gives the life period, that is the shelf life, together with the conditions of storage for listed drugs; Schedule P1 deals with pack sizes.`,`Schedule U prescribes the records of manufacture and of raw materials that a licensee must maintain; Schedule U1 is the parallel record schedule for cosmetics.`,`Schedule V lays down standards for patent and proprietary medicines, including the permitted limits of vitamins in such preparations.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Schedule J is a labelling ban, not a drug list`,text:`Schedule J lists diseases and ailments which a drug may not purport to prevent or cure on its label, for example cancer, cataract, diabetes and deafness. It is the labelling counterpart of the schedule under the Drugs and Magic Remedies Act.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Schedule K - the exemption schedule`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Household remedies sold in villages with small populations, subject to conditions.`,`Drugs supplied by a registered medical practitioner to his own patient.`,`Substances not intended for medicinal use, when labelled as such.`,`Drugs supplied to a hospital or dispensary maintained by Government, and drugs imported or manufactured for test, analysis or examination.`]}]},{topicId:`pj-schedules`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced layer is about the schedules that changed - Schedule Y and Schedule M in particular - and about the fine detail of Schedule F, D and Y that distinguishes a strong candidate.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Schedule Y and the shift to the 2019 rules`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Schedule Y historically carried the requirements and guidelines for permission to import or manufacture a new drug and for conducting clinical trials, including the phase definitions and the format of the trial protocol.`,`It defined a new drug to include a drug not previously used in the country, a new claim or new route or new dosage form for an approved drug, and a fixed dose combination not previously approved.`,`Much of this ground is now governed by the New Drugs and Clinical Trials Rules, 2019, which added timelines for approval, an ethics committee registration regime and compensation rules for trial injury.`,`For GPAT, answer Schedule Y when the question says "clinical trials and new drugs under the D and C Rules", but be aware of the 2019 rules if the question mentions ethics committee registration or compensation.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Trial phase`,`Purpose`,`Typical subjects`],rows:[[`Phase I`,`Human pharmacology, safety, tolerability, pharmacokinetics`,`Small number of healthy volunteers`],[`Phase II`,`Therapeutic exploratory, dose finding`,`Limited number of patients`],[`Phase III`,`Therapeutic confirmatory, efficacy and safety`,`Large patient groups`],[`Phase IV`,`Post-marketing surveillance, new uses, rare adverse effects`,`General patient population`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Schedule F family in detail`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Schedule F carries the requirements for the functioning and operation of a blood bank and for the preparation of blood components and blood products - donor selection, testing, storage temperature and records.`,`Schedule F1 relates to the manufacture of vaccines and sera; Schedule F2 gives standards for surgical dressings; Schedule F3 gives standards for sterilised umbilical tapes.`,`Schedule FF gives the standards for ophthalmic preparations, which is why the sterility and particulate requirements for eye drops trace back to it.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Schedule D versus Schedule K`,text:`Both are exemption schedules but they sit in different chapters. Schedule D exempts specified classes from provisions of Chapter III, which is import. Schedule K exempts specified classes from provisions of Chapter IV, which is manufacture, sale and distribution.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Schedule M revision`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Schedule M has been revised to bring Indian GMP closer to WHO GMP - it now emphasises a pharmaceutical quality system, quality risk management, product quality review, computerised system validation and stability testing responsibilities.`,`Part I covers general requirements - premises, air handling, water systems, personnel, documentation, self-inspection and complaints handling.`,`Part II gives specific requirements for sterile products, oral solid dosage forms, oral liquids, topicals, metered dose inhalers, biologicals and other categories.`,`Warehousing, quality control laboratory area and the manufacturing area must be separate and adequately sized, with defined air classes for sterile areas.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Three-line answer that covers most schedule questions`,text:`If a question names a list of drugs, think C, C1, G, H, H1 or X. If it names a standard, think F, FF, O, R, S or V. If it names a requirement of premises or records, think M, N, T or U. If it names a prohibition, think J.`}]},{topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The Pharmacy Act, 1948 regulates the profession and the practice of pharmacy. Where the Drugs and Cosmetics Act controls the product, the Pharmacy Act controls the person who handles it.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The three things the Act does`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Creates the Pharmacy Council of India, a central statutory council.`,`Requires every State to have a State Pharmacy Council with a register of pharmacists.`,`Bars anyone other than a registered pharmacist from compounding and dispensing a medical prescription.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Vocabulary`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Term`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`PCI`,`Pharmacy Council of India - the central council under the Act`],[`State Pharmacy Council`,`State body that registers pharmacists and maintains the register`],[`Education Regulations`,`The minimum standards of education framed by PCI for the pharmacy qualification`],[`Registered pharmacist`,`A person whose name is entered in a State register of pharmacists`],[`Registration Tribunal`,`Body that prepares the first register in a State`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The single most asked provision`,text:`Section 42 - no person other than a registered pharmacist shall compound, prepare, mix or dispense any medicine on the prescription of a medical practitioner. Contravention attracts imprisonment which may extend to six months, or fine, or both.`},{kind:`para`,text:`The minimum qualification for registration as a pharmacist in India is the Diploma in Pharmacy from a PCI-approved institution, which includes a period of practical training; a degree in pharmacy also qualifies a person for registration.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`PCI is not the licensing authority`,text:`PCI sets education standards and approves courses. It does not issue drug licences and it does not inspect chemist shops - that is the State Drugs Controller under the D and C Act.`}]},{topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Exam questions cluster on the composition of PCI, the approval and withdrawal of courses, and the mechanics of registration. Learn the categories of members rather than trying to memorise a single total, because State representation makes the total vary.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Composition of the Pharmacy Council of India`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Category`,`Broad description`],rows:[[`Elected teachers`,`Six members elected from among teachers of pharmacy subjects in universities and colleges`],[`Elected pharmacists`,`Six members elected by the registered pharmacists of the States from among themselves`],[`Medical representation`,`One member elected from among themselves by the members of the central medical council`],[`Ex-officio`,`Director General of Health Services, Drugs Controller General (India) and the Director of the Central Drugs Laboratory, or their nominees`],[`Central nominees`,`Eight members nominated by the Central Government, of whom at least four must be registered pharmacists`],[`State nominees`,`One representative of each State, who is a registered pharmacist, nominated by that State Government`]],caption:`A President and Vice-President are elected by the members from among themselves; the Registrar acts as Secretary.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The term of an elected or nominated member is generally five years, or until the successor is chosen.`,`PCI functions through an Executive Committee and other committees it constitutes.`,`PCI maintains a Central Register of Pharmacists compiled from the State registers.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Education regulations and course approval`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`PCI frames Education Regulations prescribing the minimum standard of education required for qualification as a pharmacist - staff, admission requirements, course content, practical training and examination.`,`An institution seeking approval applies and is inspected; approved courses and examinations are notified.`,`If standards fall, the Council can recommend withdrawal of approval after giving the authority an opportunity to explain, and the Central Government decides.`,`The Diploma in Pharmacy course includes supervised practical training in a hospital or a licensed pharmacy in addition to classroom study.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Registration of pharmacists`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The first register in a State is prepared by a Registration Tribunal, which admitted persons on the basis of qualification or of prior practice as a compounder or dispenser.`,`After the first register, entry requires the approved qualification - the Diploma or Degree in Pharmacy from an approved institution.`,`A registered pharmacist of one State may seek registration in another State on production of the certificate, subject to the State rules.`,`Names may be removed from the register for entry obtained by fraud or misrepresentation, or for professional misconduct, after an opportunity of being heard; restoration is possible.`,`Renewal of registration at the prescribed interval with the prescribed fee is required to keep the entry live.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Reciprocity and the Central Register`,text:`The Central Register maintained by PCI is a compilation of the State registers, which is what makes reciprocal recognition between States workable. Questions sometimes ask who maintains the Central Register - the answer is the Pharmacy Council of India, not the State Council.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Offences under the Act`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Offence`,`Nature of penalty`],rows:[[`Dispensing by a person who is not a registered pharmacist`,`Imprisonment up to six months, or fine, or both`],[`Falsely claiming to be a registered pharmacist`,`Fine, enhanced on subsequent conviction`],[`Failure to surrender a cancelled registration certificate`,`Fine as prescribed`]]}]},{topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At the top end you should be able to place the Pharmacy Act next to the D and C Act, explain the practice regulations that flesh out professional conduct, and handle the exceptions to Section 42.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Two Acts, two jobs`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Aspect`,`Pharmacy Act 1948`,`Drugs and Cosmetics Act 1940`],rows:[[`Object`,`Regulate the profession and the practitioner`,`Regulate the product and the trade`],[`Central body`,`Pharmacy Council of India`,`DTAB, DCC, CDSCO`],[`State body`,`State Pharmacy Council`,`State Licensing Authority and Drug Inspectors`],[`Key instrument`,`Register of pharmacists`,`Licence to import, manufacture or sell`],[`Signature offence`,`Dispensing by an unregistered person`,`Manufacture or sale of a spurious or substandard drug`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Exceptions and limits of Section 42`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The bar applies to compounding, preparing, mixing or dispensing on a medical prescription - not to every act of selling a packaged medicine, which is governed by the sale licence conditions under the D and C Rules.`,`A registered medical practitioner dispensing to his own patient is treated differently, and the D and C Rules contain a parallel exemption in Schedule K.`,`A person working under the personal supervision of a registered pharmacist is not thereby a registered pharmacist - supervision is what makes the transaction lawful, and it must be real, not nominal.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Pharmacy Practice Regulations`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The Pharmacy Practice Regulations, 2015 made by PCI set out the code of conduct, duties towards the patient and the prescriber, prescription handling and record keeping, and the requirement to display the registration certificate.`,`They cover the pharmacist’s role in patient counselling, dispensing accuracy, and maintenance of a prescription and patient medication record.`,`They also address confidentiality, professional advertisement restrictions and the requirement of continuing education.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Who approves a pharmacy college?`,text:`For the pharmacy qualification to count for registration, PCI approval under the Pharmacy Act is what matters. AICTE approval and university affiliation address technical education and degree award respectively - they do not by themselves make a graduate registrable.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Numbers worth carrying`,text:`Six teacher members and six pharmacist members elected, eight Central Government nominees of whom at least four are registered pharmacists, one representative per State, five-year term, and a six-month maximum imprisonment under Section 42.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Where a question gives a total membership figure for PCI, treat it with care - the number of State nominees changes as States are reorganised, so the categories are the reliable answer.`}]},{topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This topic bundles four allied laws: the NDPS Act 1985 for narcotics, the Drugs and Magic Remedies Act 1954 for advertisements, the Medicinal and Toilet Preparations Act 1955 for excise on alcohol-containing preparations, and the Poisons Act 1919.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`NDPS Act, 1985 in one screen`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Full title: Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985.`,`It replaced the older opium and dangerous drugs laws with a single consolidated statute.`,`Narcotic drug means coca leaf, cannabis or hemp, opium, poppy straw, and all manufactured drugs.`,`Psychotropic substance means a substance or preparation listed in the Schedule to the Act.`,`The Act prohibits cultivation, production, manufacture, possession, sale, purchase, transport, import, export and use except for medical or scientific purposes under licence.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Bhang`,text:`Cannabis or hemp under the Act means charas, ganja and any mixture or drink prepared from them. The leaves and seeds of the plant, when not accompanied by the tops, are outside that definition - which is why bhang is treated differently from ganja.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The other three Acts in one line each`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Act`,`What it does`],rows:[[`Drugs and Magic Remedies (Objectionable Advertisements) Act, 1954`,`Bans advertising drugs for listed diseases and bans magic remedy claims`],[`Medicinal and Toilet Preparations (Excise Duties) Act, 1955`,`Levies excise duty on preparations containing alcohol or narcotic substances`],[`Poisons Act, 1919`,`Lets Governments regulate the possession for sale and the sale of listed poisons`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Do not mix the Acts up`,text:`A false claim on a label is D and C Act misbranding. A newspaper advertisement claiming a cure for diabetes is the Magic Remedies Act. A tincture containing alcohol attracts the Medicinal and Toilet Preparations Act. Morphine stock control is NDPS plus Schedule X.`}]},{topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The NDPS penalty structure is graded by quantity, and the Magic Remedies Act is examined through its schedule of diseases and its four prohibited purposes. Both are high-yield.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`NDPS authorities`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The Central Government may appoint a Narcotics Commissioner and other officers, and may constitute an authority for the purposes of the Act - the Narcotics Control Bureau is the apex coordinating agency so constituted.`,`The Narcotics Commissioner has the power to superintend the cultivation of the opium poppy and the production of opium.`,`A National Fund for Control of Drug Abuse is created for combating illicit traffic and for identification, treatment and rehabilitation of addicts.`,`Officers of central and state departments such as excise, police, customs and revenue intelligence are empowered to enter, search, seize and arrest.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Graded punishment by quantity`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Quantity involved`,`Broad punishment`],rows:[[`Small quantity`,`Rigorous imprisonment up to one year, or fine up to ten thousand rupees, or both`],[`More than small but less than commercial`,`Rigorous imprisonment up to ten years and fine up to one lakh rupees`],[`Commercial quantity`,`Rigorous imprisonment not less than ten years extending to twenty years and fine not less than one lakh rupees`]],caption:`Small and commercial quantities for each substance are fixed by Central Government notification and have been revised.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Offences under the Act are cognizable and non-bailable, and bail is subject to stringent conditions.`,`Consumption of a narcotic drug carries a lesser punishment than trafficking, and an addict who volunteers for treatment at a recognised centre may claim immunity from prosecution for consumption.`,`Repeat offences involving commercial quantity attract enhanced punishment, and the Act contains provisions for forfeiture of property derived from illicit traffic.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Drugs and Magic Remedies Act, 1954`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Section 3 prohibits advertisement of a drug suggesting its use for the procurement of miscarriage or prevention of conception, for maintenance or improvement of the capacity for sexual pleasure, for correction of menstrual disorder, or for the diagnosis, cure, mitigation, treatment or prevention of any disease listed in the Schedule.`,`Section 4 prohibits misleading advertisements of drugs.`,`Section 5 prohibits advertisement of magic remedies for the same conditions.`,`Section 7 provides imprisonment up to six months or fine or both on first conviction, and up to one year or fine or both on subsequent conviction.`,`Section 14 exempts advertisements sent confidentially to registered medical practitioners, treatises dealing with the subject, and labels or literature complying with the D and C Act.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Diseases named in the Schedule include cancer, cataract, deafness, diabetes, epilepsy, glaucoma, goitre, high and low blood pressure, heart disease, leprosy, leucoderma, obesity, paralysis, sexual impotence, sterility in women, tuberculosis and venereal diseases.`,`The Schedule also covers claims to change the stature of a person and claims about fevers in general and female diseases in general.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Four prohibited purposes`,text:`Miscarriage, Menstrual disorder, Manhood (sexual pleasure) and Malady on the Schedule - the four Ms of Section 3.`}]},{topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced material here is the excise structure for alcoholic medicinal preparations and the finer NDPS points that separate a confident answer from a guess.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Medicinal and Toilet Preparations (Excise Duties) Act, 1955`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`It applies to medicinal and toilet preparations containing alcohol, opium, Indian hemp or other narcotic drug or narcotic.`,`Alcohol for the purpose of the Act means ethyl alcohol of any strength and purity, whatever the source.`,`Duty is levied by the Union but collected and retained by the State in which the preparation is manufactured, which is why the State excise department administers it.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Concept`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Bonded manufacture`,`Made in a bonded laboratory using duty-free alcohol under bond and excise supervision`],[`Non-bonded manufacture`,`Made using alcohol on which duty has already been paid; less official supervision`],[`Restricted preparation`,`Contains alcohol and is capable of being misused as an ordinary alcoholic beverage`],[`Unrestricted preparation`,`Contains enough medicament to make it unsuitable for use as a beverage`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Bonded versus non-bonded`,text:`Bonded manufacture uses duty-free spirit and is preferred for large-scale production of preparations with high alcohol content, because duty is paid only on the finished dutiable goods that are actually cleared. Non-bonded manufacture uses duty-paid alcohol and suits small-scale work.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Spirits you must be able to name`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Spirit`,`Description`],rows:[[`Rectified spirit`,`Purified ethyl alcohol of about 90 per cent v/v strength`],[`Absolute alcohol`,`Ethyl alcohol containing essentially no water`],[`Denatured or industrial spirit`,`Alcohol rendered unfit for human consumption by added denaturants`],[`Proof spirit`,`The reference standard against which alcoholic strength is expressed in excise practice`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`NDPS fine points`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The Act provides for the death penalty for certain repeat offences involving very large quantities; after amendment the court has the discretion to award a long term of imprisonment instead.`,`Where a seizure lies between small and commercial quantity, the intermediate punishment applies - candidates commonly wrongly jump to the commercial slab.`,`Manufacture of morphine, pethidine, methadone and similar essential narcotics for medical use is lawful under licence, and the amended framework recognises essential narcotic drugs to improve access for pain relief.`,`A registered medical practitioner and a licensed chemist handling narcotic preparations must also satisfy Schedule X requirements under the D and C Rules - the two regimes operate together.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Poisons Act, 1919`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`It empowers the State Government to make rules regulating the possession for sale and the sale, whether wholesale or retail, of any specified poison.`,`It empowers the Central Government to regulate the import of specified poisons.`,`Rules typically require a licence, a poisons register, secure storage separate from other stock, and distinctive labelling.`,`Penalties include imprisonment and fine, and the substance may be confiscated.`]}]},{topicId:`pj-price-control`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Drug price control in India works through the Drugs (Prices Control) Order, which is issued not under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act but under the Essential Commodities Act, 1955. The National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority implements it.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The chain of authority`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Essential Commodities Act, 1955 gives the Central Government power to control production, supply and distribution of essential commodities, drugs among them.`,`Under that power the Central Government issues the Drugs (Prices Control) Order - DPCO.`,`The National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority (NPPA) fixes, revises and enforces the prices under the Order.`,`NPPA works under the Department of Pharmaceuticals, Ministry of Chemicals and Fertilizers.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Term`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Scheduled formulation`,`A formulation listed in the First Schedule to the DPCO - it gets a ceiling price`],[`Non-scheduled formulation`,`Not listed - the maker sets the price but the annual increase is capped`],[`Ceiling price`,`The maximum price at which a scheduled formulation may be sold`],[`Retail price`,`The price fixed for a new drug or specified formulation`],[`NLEM`,`National List of Essential Medicines - the basis of the DPCO First Schedule`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Two dates and two names`,text:`DPCO is issued under the Essential Commodities Act 1955. The current order is the DPCO 2013, which replaced the cost-based DPCO 1995 with market-based ceiling pricing. NPPA is the implementing authority.`},{kind:`para`,text:`The other regulator you must not confuse with NPPA is CDSCO. NPPA controls price; CDSCO controls quality, approval and import. They are different ministries and different statutes.`}]},{topicId:`pj-price-control`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core here is the ceiling price formula of DPCO 2013, the annual revision rules, and the structure and functions of NPPA and CDSCO.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Ceiling price under DPCO 2013`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`P(ceiling) = Average Price to Retailer x (1 + M / 100)`,caption:`P(ceiling) = ceiling price of the scheduled formulation. Average Price to Retailer = simple average of the price to retailer of all brands of that formulation having a market share at or above the notified threshold of total market turnover. M = notified retailer margin, taken as 16 per cent.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The market share threshold used for including a brand in the average is one per cent of the total market turnover of that formulation.`,`The average is a simple arithmetic average of the qualifying brands, not a sales-weighted average - this is a favourite distractor.`,`Ceiling prices are exclusive of applicable tax, which is added to arrive at the maximum retail price.`,`A manufacturer may sell below the ceiling price; selling above it attracts recovery of the overcharged amount with interest and penalty.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Price revision rules`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class of formulation`,`Revision rule`],rows:[[`Scheduled formulation`,`Ceiling price revised annually in line with the Wholesale Price Index`],[`Non-scheduled formulation`,`Maker may raise the price by not more than ten per cent in the preceding twelve months`],[`New drug under a patent regime`,`Price fixed by the Government on the terms notified for such drugs`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Ten per cent is annual, not per revision`,text:`For a non-scheduled formulation the cap is a maximum increase of ten per cent over the preceding twelve months, taken as a whole. Several small increases within a year are added together against the same cap.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The older cost-based formula`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`R.P. = (M.C. + C.C. + P.M. + P.C.) x (1 + MAPE / 100) + ED`,caption:`R.P. = retail price. M.C. = material cost. C.C. = conversion cost. P.M. = cost of packing material. P.C. = packing charges. MAPE = maximum allowable post-manufacturing expenses. ED = excise duty where applicable. This cost-plus approach belonged to the DPCO 1995 regime.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Functions of NPPA`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Fix and revise ceiling prices of scheduled formulations and retail prices where required.`,`Monitor the prices of both scheduled and non-scheduled formulations.`,`Recover amounts overcharged from consumers, with interest.`,`Monitor availability and shortages of medicines and take action to ensure supply.`,`Collect and maintain data on production, exports, imports and market share of the industry, and advise the Government on pricing policy.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Paragraph 19 power`,text:`DPCO 2013 contains an extraordinary power under which the Government may fix the ceiling or retail price of any drug, including a non-scheduled drug, in the public interest and in circumstances it considers extraordinary. This power has been used for cardiac stents and knee implants.`}]},{topicId:`pj-price-control`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions ask you to link the National List of Essential Medicines to the DPCO First Schedule, and to lay out the CDSCO structure with the split of functions between the Centre and the States.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`NLEM and the First Schedule`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The National List of Essential Medicines is prepared by the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare using criteria of disease burden, proven efficacy and safety, cost effectiveness and suitability for the Indian health system.`,`India first published an NLEM in 1996 and has revised it in 2003, 2011, 2015 and 2022.`,`The 2022 revision lists 384 medicines arranged by therapeutic category, with the level of healthcare - primary, secondary or tertiary - indicated against each.`,`When NLEM is revised, the DPCO First Schedule is amended to match, which is how a medicine moves into price control.`,`A medicine listed for a specified strength and dosage form is controlled in that strength and form; other strengths of the same molecule may fall outside control.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`CDSCO structure`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Level`,`Body or officer`,`Main functions`],rows:[[`Apex`,`Drugs Controller General (India)`,`Head of CDSCO, approval of new drugs and clinical trials, import licensing`],[`Parent department`,`Directorate General of Health Services, Ministry of Health and Family Welfare`,`Administrative control of CDSCO`],[`Field`,`Zonal and sub-zonal offices, port offices`,`Inspection, sampling, import clearance at ports and airports`],[`Laboratories`,`Central Drugs Laboratory and central testing laboratories`,`Statutory testing and referee analysis`],[`State`,`State Licensing Authority and Drug Inspectors`,`Manufacturing and sale licences, market surveillance, prosecution`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Central versus State licensing`,text:`Manufacturing and sale licences are issued by the State Licensing Authority. The Centre, acting as the Central Licence Approving Authority, gives concurrence for specified classes such as vaccines, sera, blood products and large volume parenterals, and it alone approves new drugs, clinical trials and imports.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Enforcement of price orders`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`The manufacturer must issue a price list in the prescribed form to dealers and to the Government, and display it as required.`,`Overcharging attracts recovery of the excess amount collected from consumers, together with interest, from the date of overcharging.`,`Continued default can lead to action under the Essential Commodities Act, which carries penal consequences beyond mere recovery.`,`A ceiling price notified by NPPA is binding on every manufacturer and marketer of that formulation, including new entrants.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Three-body separation`,text:`PCI regulates the pharmacist, CDSCO regulates the drug, NPPA regulates the price. A question naming any one function should let you name the correct body immediately.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Note that price control figures such as the retailer margin and the market share threshold are set by notification and can be revised; the structure of the formula is what stays constant and is what is examined.`}]}],t=[{id:`q-pj-dc-act-1`,topicId:`pj-dc-act`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The Central Drugs Laboratory under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act is located at`,options:[`New Delhi`,`Mumbai`,`Kolkata`,`Chennai`],answer:2,explanation:`The Central Drugs Laboratory, constituted under Section 6, functions at Kolkata and acts as the statutory referee laboratory. New Delhi is the headquarters of CDSCO and of the DCGI, which is the usual distractor here, but it is not the CDL.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pj-dc-act-2`,topicId:`pj-dc-act`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The technical advisory body that advises the Central and State Governments on technical matters arising out of the Drugs and Cosmetics Act is the`,options:[`Drugs Technical Advisory Board`,`Drugs Consultative Committee`,`Pharmacy Council of India`,`National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority`],answer:0,explanation:`DTAB, constituted under Section 5, is the technical advisory board and is consulted before rules are framed. The Drugs Consultative Committee is also advisory but its specific purpose is to secure uniformity of administration of the Act between the Centre and the States.`},{id:`q-pj-dc-act-3`,topicId:`pj-dc-act`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A tablet is manufactured by firm A but is labelled to show that it was manufactured by the well known firm B. Under the Act this drug is`,options:[`Misbranded only`,`Adulterated`,`Spurious`,`Not of standard quality`],answer:2,explanation:`Falsely claiming to be the product of another manufacturer is the classic instance of a spurious drug under Section 17B. Misbranding under Section 17 covers false or misleading labelling about the drug itself, whereas here the fraud is about the identity of the maker, which the Act treats far more seriously.`},{id:`q-pj-dc-act-4`,topicId:`pj-dc-act`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug consisting in part of a filthy or decomposed substance, or prepared under insanitary conditions, is classified as`,options:[`Spurious`,`Misbranded`,`Patent and proprietary`,`Adulterated`],answer:3,explanation:`Section 17A defines an adulterated drug in exactly these terms - filthy, putrid or decomposed substance, insanitary conditions, harmful container or unsafe colour. Spurious concerns the identity of the product or manufacturer, not the physical condition of the material.`},{id:`q-pj-dc-act-5`,topicId:`pj-dc-act`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Under the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, the licence to manufacture drugs falling in Schedule C and C1 is granted in`,options:[`Form 20`,`Form 25`,`Form 28`,`Form 10`],answer:2,explanation:`Form 28 is the manufacturing licence for Schedule C, C1 and X drugs, applied for in Form 27. Form 25 is the manufacturing licence for drugs other than those specified classes, which is the distractor most often chosen; Form 20 is a retail sale licence and Form 10 is an import licence.`},{id:`q-pj-dc-act-6`,topicId:`pj-dc-act`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`When a Drug Inspector takes a sample for test and the sample is divided, the portion produced before the court is used to`,options:[`Establish the manufacturing date of the batch`,`Be sent to the Central Drugs Laboratory if the Government Analyst report is challenged`,`Be returned to the manufacturer after the trial`,`Be tested by a private laboratory chosen by the accused`],answer:1,explanation:`Under Section 25 the accused may notify the court that he intends to challenge the Government Analyst report, whereupon the court sends its retained portion to the Central Drugs Laboratory, and the CDL certificate is conclusive evidence. The accused cannot substitute a private laboratory of his own choosing for this statutory referee test.`},{id:`q-pj-dc-act-7`,topicId:`pj-dc-act`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`For manufacture or sale of a spurious drug that is likely to cause death or grievous hurt, the Act after the 2008 amendment prescribes imprisonment of`,options:[`Not less than one year extending to three years`,`Not less than three years extending to five years`,`Not less than ten years extending to imprisonment for life`,`Up to six months only, with fine`],answer:2,explanation:`The gravest slab under Section 27 carries a minimum of ten years, extendable to imprisonment for life, together with a heavy fine, and these offences were made cognizable and non-bailable. The three-to-five-year slab is the punishment for adulterated or spurious drugs that are not likely to cause death or grievous hurt.`,tag:`Penalties`},{id:`q-pj-dc-act-8`,topicId:`pj-dc-act`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The provision commonly used by the Central Government to prohibit the manufacture and sale of an irrational fixed dose combination in the public interest is`,options:[`Section 26A`,`Section 18(a)(i)`,`Section 33P`,`Section 16`],answer:0,explanation:`Section 26A empowers the Central Government, by notification and in the public interest, to prohibit the manufacture, sale or distribution of a drug or cosmetic, and it is the section relied on for banning irrational fixed dose combinations. Section 18 is the general prohibition on substandard and unlicensed drugs, Section 33P allows directions to States, and Section 16 fixes standards of quality.`},{id:`q-pj-schedules-1`,topicId:`pj-schedules`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The minimum equipment required for the efficient running of a pharmacy is listed in`,options:[`Schedule M`,`Schedule N`,`Schedule P`,`Schedule U`],answer:1,explanation:`Schedule N gives the list of minimum equipment for a pharmacy, including balances, measures, water bath, refrigerator and the reference books. Schedule M is the GMP schedule for manufacturing premises, which is the commonest wrong choice because both concern equipment and premises.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pj-schedules-2`,topicId:`pj-schedules`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Life period of drugs, that is shelf life together with the conditions of storage, is dealt with in`,options:[`Schedule V`,`Schedule O`,`Schedule P`,`Schedule J`],answer:2,explanation:`Schedule P prescribes the life period and storage conditions of listed drugs. Schedule V deals with standards for patent and proprietary medicines, Schedule O with disinfectant fluids and Schedule J with diseases a drug may not claim to cure.`},{id:`q-pj-schedules-3`,topicId:`pj-schedules`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A pharmacist must maintain a separate bound register of sales, retained for three years, for drugs falling under`,options:[`Schedule H`,`Schedule G`,`Schedule H1`,`Schedule C1`],answer:2,explanation:`Schedule H1 requires the sale to be recorded in a separate register giving the patient and prescriber details, and that register is retained for three years. Ordinary Schedule H prescription records are kept for two years, so the distinguishing features of H1 are the separate register, the longer retention and the boxed warning on the label.`},{id:`q-pj-schedules-4`,topicId:`pj-schedules`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Sera, vaccines, toxins, antitoxins and insulin preparations fall under`,options:[`Schedule C`,`Schedule C1`,`Schedule F`,`Schedule D`],answer:0,explanation:`Schedule C covers biological and special products such as sera, vaccines, toxins, antitoxins, insulin and hormone preparations. Schedule C1 is for other special products such as preparations of digitalis and ergot; Schedule F sets requirements for blood banks and certain sterile articles.`},{id:`q-pj-schedules-5`,topicId:`pj-schedules`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The caution "it is dangerous to take this preparation except under medical supervision" must appear on the label of drugs listed in`,options:[`Schedule H`,`Schedule X`,`Schedule G`,`Schedule K`],answer:2,explanation:`Schedule G drugs carry that caution statement, and antineoplastic agents are the classic examples. Schedule H drugs carry the red Rx symbol and the statement that they are to be sold on the prescription of a registered medical practitioner, which is a different requirement.`},{id:`q-pj-schedules-6`,topicId:`pj-schedules`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which schedule exempts specified classes of drugs from provisions of Chapter IV, such as household remedies sold in small villages and drugs supplied by a registered medical practitioner to his own patient?`,options:[`Schedule D`,`Schedule K`,`Schedule J`,`Schedule B`],answer:1,explanation:`Schedule K is the Chapter IV exemption schedule and contains exactly these categories. Schedule D also grants exemptions but only from Chapter III, which governs import, and confusing the two chapters is the standard error.`},{id:`q-pj-schedules-7`,topicId:`pj-schedules`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A prescription for a Schedule X drug must be`,options:[`Written in duplicate, one copy retained by the licensee for two years`,`Written in triplicate, one copy sent to the licensing authority`,`Retained by the prescriber, with the pharmacist keeping only a register entry`,`Repeatable up to three times within six months`],answer:0,explanation:`Schedule X requires the prescription to be in duplicate, with one copy retained by the licensee for two years, and the sale is possible only under a special Schedule X licence. Schedule X prescriptions are not repeatable, which rules out the option offering repeats.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pj-schedules-8`,topicId:`pj-schedules`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Requirements and guidelines for permission to import or manufacture a new drug and to conduct clinical trials were classically laid down in Schedule Y. Which statement best reflects the current position?`,options:[`Schedule Y was replaced by Schedule M in the same rules`,`Much of the subject is now governed by the New Drugs and Clinical Trials Rules, 2019`,`Clinical trials are now regulated only by the Pharmacy Act`,`Schedule Y now governs only bioequivalence studies of cosmetics`],answer:1,explanation:`The New Drugs and Clinical Trials Rules, 2019 now carry much of the clinical trial and new drug approval framework, adding approval timelines, ethics committee registration and compensation for trial injury. Schedule M is the GMP schedule and has nothing to do with trials, and the Pharmacy Act regulates practitioners, not trials.`},{id:`q-pj-pharmacy-act-1`,topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Under the Pharmacy Act, 1948 the section that bars any person other than a registered pharmacist from compounding or dispensing a medical prescription is`,options:[`Section 12`,`Section 26`,`Section 42`,`Section 33`],answer:2,explanation:`Section 42 is the bar on dispensing by unregistered persons, and contravention carries imprisonment which may extend to six months, or fine, or both. Section 12 concerns approval of courses of study and Section 26 concerns registers of pharmacists.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pj-pharmacy-act-2`,topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The minimum educational qualification prescribed for registration as a pharmacist in India is`,options:[`Diploma in Pharmacy from a PCI approved institution`,`Bachelor of Pharmacy only`,`A pass in the school leaving examination with two years of experience`,`A certificate course in dispensing`],answer:0,explanation:`The Diploma in Pharmacy from an institution approved by the Pharmacy Council of India, including the prescribed practical training, is the minimum qualification for registration; a degree in pharmacy also qualifies. Experience-based entry belonged only to the first register prepared by the Registration Tribunal and is no longer a route.`},{id:`q-pj-pharmacy-act-3`,topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The Central Register of Pharmacists is maintained by`,options:[`The State Pharmacy Council of the pharmacist’s home State`,`The Pharmacy Council of India`,`The Drugs Controller General (India)`,`The Registration Tribunal`],answer:1,explanation:`PCI maintains the Central Register, compiled from the entries in the State registers, and it is the basis of reciprocal recognition between States. The State Council maintains its own State register but not the central one, and the Registration Tribunal only prepared the first register.`},{id:`q-pj-pharmacy-act-4`,topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following is an ex-officio member of the Pharmacy Council of India?`,options:[`The President of the Indian Pharmaceutical Association`,`The Director General of Health Services`,`The Chairman of the National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority`,`The Secretary of the University Grants Commission`],answer:1,explanation:`The Director General of Health Services, the Drugs Controller General (India) and the Director of the Central Drugs Laboratory, or their nominees, sit on PCI ex-officio. NPPA is a pricing body with no seat on PCI, and professional associations have no ex-officio place under the Act.`},{id:`q-pj-pharmacy-act-5`,topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The Pharmacy Act, 1948 requires the Central Council to include at least one teacher of each of four named subjects. Those subjects are`,options:[`Pharmaceutics, pharmacology, microbiology and biochemistry`,`Pharmaceutical chemistry, pharmacy, pharmacology and pharmacognosy`,`Pharmaceutical chemistry, pharmaceutics, jurisprudence and pharmacognosy`,`Pharmacology, pathology, pharmacognosy and pharmaceutics`],answer:1,explanation:`The composition provisions guarantee academic representation from the four classical pillars of the pharmacy curriculum — pharmaceutical chemistry, pharmacy, pharmacology and pharmacognosy — so that no one discipline dominates the body that sets the Education Regulations. Microbiology, biochemistry, jurisprudence and pathology are all taught in the course but none is named in this requirement.`},{id:`q-pj-pharmacy-act-6`,topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Prescribing the minimum standard of education required for qualification as a pharmacist is the function of`,options:[`The State Pharmacy Council`,`The Pharmacy Council of India`,`The State Licensing Authority`,`The Drugs Technical Advisory Board`],answer:1,explanation:`PCI frames the Education Regulations that set the minimum standard of education, and it approves courses and examinations for that purpose. The State Pharmacy Council registers pharmacists but does not set the syllabus, and DTAB is a technical body under an entirely different Act.`},{id:`q-pj-pharmacy-act-7`,topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A shop assistant who is not a registered pharmacist compounds a mixture from a doctor’s prescription while the registered pharmacist employed at the shop is away for the day. The correct legal position is that`,options:[`There is no offence because the shop holds a valid sale licence`,`There is no offence because the assistant acted under a standing instruction`,`Section 42 of the Pharmacy Act is contravened, punishable with imprisonment up to six months or fine or both`,`Only the D and C Act labelling provisions are attracted`],answer:2,explanation:`Section 42 forbids compounding or dispensing on a medical prescription by anyone other than a registered pharmacist, and personal supervision must be actual, not notional, so a standing instruction is no defence. A valid sale licence under the D and C Act addresses the trade in drugs and does not cure the personal disqualification under the Pharmacy Act.`},{id:`q-pj-pharmacy-act-8`,topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Approval of a pharmacy course for the purpose of registration of its graduates as pharmacists is withdrawn when`,options:[`The Council recommends withdrawal after an opportunity to explain, and the Central Government decides`,`The State Pharmacy Council passes a resolution to that effect`,`The university withdraws affiliation, automatically ending approval`,`The Drugs Controller General cancels the institution’s licence`],answer:0,explanation:`Approval of courses is a PCI function, and withdrawal follows a report of falling standards, an opportunity to the authority concerned to explain, a Council recommendation and a decision by the Central Government. University affiliation and drug licensing are separate matters and neither controls approval under the Pharmacy Act.`},{id:`q-pj-ndps-and-others-1`,topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act was enacted in`,options:[`1940`,`1954`,`1985`,`1995`],answer:2,explanation:`The NDPS Act was enacted in 1985 and consolidated the earlier opium and dangerous drugs laws. 1940 is the D and C Act, 1954 is the Drugs and Magic Remedies Act and 1995 is a DPCO year, all of which appear as distractors in this question.`},{id:`q-pj-ndps-and-others-2`,topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Advertising a proprietary medicine as a cure for cataract in a newspaper contravenes the`,options:[`Drugs and Magic Remedies (Objectionable Advertisements) Act, 1954`,`Poisons Act, 1919`,`Medicinal and Toilet Preparations Act, 1955`,`Essential Commodities Act, 1955`],answer:0,explanation:`Cataract is one of the diseases named in the Schedule to the Drugs and Magic Remedies Act, and Section 3 prohibits advertising a drug as a treatment for it. The Poisons Act deals with sale of poisons and the Medicinal and Toilet Preparations Act with excise duty on alcoholic preparations, neither of which touches advertising.`},{id:`q-pj-ndps-and-others-3`,topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Under the NDPS Act, the definition of cannabis or hemp does not include`,options:[`Charas`,`Ganja`,`The leaves and seeds when not accompanied by the tops`,`A drink prepared from charas`],answer:2,explanation:`The Act defines cannabis as charas, ganja and any mixture or drink prepared from them, and expressly leaves out the leaves and seeds when they are not accompanied by the flowering or fruiting tops. That exclusion is why bhang is handled outside the ganja provisions of the Act.`},{id:`q-pj-ndps-and-others-4`,topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Under the NDPS Act, an offence involving a commercial quantity attracts rigorous imprisonment of`,options:[`Up to one year`,`Up to ten years`,`Not less than ten years and up to twenty years`,`Not less than twenty years and up to thirty years`],answer:2,explanation:`Commercial quantity attracts a mandatory minimum of ten years, extendable to twenty years, with a substantial fine. The up to ten years slab belongs to quantities greater than small but less than commercial, which is the distractor most candidates fall for.`,tag:`Penalties`},{id:`q-pj-ndps-and-others-5`,topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Section 3 of the Drugs and Magic Remedies Act prohibits advertisement of a drug for all of the following purposes EXCEPT`,options:[`Procurement of miscarriage`,`Maintenance or improvement of capacity for sexual pleasure`,`Correction of menstrual disorder in women`,`Relief of an ordinary headache`],answer:3,explanation:`Section 3 lists miscarriage or prevention of conception, sexual pleasure, menstrual disorder and any disease named in the Schedule. An ordinary headache is not in that list, so advertising a simple analgesic for headache is not caught by Section 3, although a misleading claim would still be caught by Section 4.`},{id:`q-pj-ndps-and-others-6`,topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A tincture prepared in a bonded laboratory using duty-free rectified spirit is being manufactured under`,options:[`Non-bonded manufacture`,`Bonded manufacture`,`Loan licence manufacture`,`Repacking licence`],answer:1,explanation:`Bonded manufacture under the Medicinal and Toilet Preparations (Excise Duties) Act means production in a bonded laboratory using alcohol on which duty has not been paid, under excise supervision and bond. Non-bonded manufacture uses duty-paid alcohol and carries far less official supervision.`},{id:`q-pj-ndps-and-others-7`,topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Under the Medicinal and Toilet Preparations (Excise Duties) Act, a preparation is classed as restricted when it`,options:[`Contains a Schedule H drug`,`Is capable of being misused as an ordinary alcoholic beverage`,`Requires a Schedule X licence for sale`,`Contains more than one active ingredient`],answer:1,explanation:`Restricted preparations are those containing alcohol which are capable of being consumed as ordinary alcoholic beverages, and they attract the higher rate of duty. Unrestricted preparations contain enough medicament to make beverage use unattractive, so the classification turns on beverage potential and not on the prescription schedule of the active ingredient.`},{id:`q-pj-ndps-and-others-8`,topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which statement about enforcement under the NDPS Act is correct?`,options:[`Offences are non-cognizable and bailable as a rule`,`An addict who volunteers for treatment at a recognised centre may be given immunity from prosecution for consumption`,`Consumption attracts the same punishment as trafficking in a commercial quantity`,`The Act applies only to substances listed in the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules`],answer:1,explanation:`The Act contains an immunity provision for an addict who voluntarily seeks treatment at a recognised centre, in respect of the offence of consumption. NDPS offences are cognizable and non-bailable with stringent bail conditions, and consumption is punished far less severely than trafficking, so the other options are wrong.`},{id:`q-pj-price-control-1`,topicId:`pj-price-control`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The Drugs (Prices Control) Order is issued under the`,options:[`Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940`,`Essential Commodities Act, 1955`,`Pharmacy Act, 1948`,`Companies Act, 2013`],answer:1,explanation:`DPCO is an order made under Section 3 of the Essential Commodities Act, 1955, which is why price control is administratively separate from the drug quality law. The Drugs and Cosmetics Act governs quality, licensing and approval but contains no price fixing power.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pj-price-control-2`,topicId:`pj-price-control`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The authority that fixes and revises the ceiling prices of scheduled formulations in India is`,options:[`CDSCO`,`NPPA`,`DTAB`,`PCI`],answer:1,explanation:`The National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority, working under the Department of Pharmaceuticals, fixes and revises prices and enforces the DPCO. CDSCO is the drug regulatory body for approval, import and quality and has no role in price fixation.`},{id:`q-pj-price-control-3`,topicId:`pj-price-control`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Under DPCO 2013 the ceiling price of a scheduled formulation is arrived at from the average price to retailer of qualifying brands by adding a retailer margin of`,options:[`8 per cent`,`10 per cent`,`16 per cent`,`20 per cent`],answer:2,explanation:`The notified margin added to the average price to retailer is 16 per cent, giving P(ceiling) = average price to retailer multiplied by 1.16. Ten per cent is the annual increase cap for non-scheduled formulations and is the distractor that traps most candidates.`},{id:`q-pj-price-control-4`,topicId:`pj-price-control`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A manufacturer of a non-scheduled formulation may increase its maximum retail price by not more than`,options:[`5 per cent in the preceding twelve months`,`10 per cent in the preceding twelve months`,`16 per cent in the preceding twelve months`,`Any amount, since non-scheduled drugs are wholly free`],answer:1,explanation:`Non-scheduled formulations are under price monitoring, and the increase is capped at ten per cent over the preceding twelve months taken as a whole. They are not wholly free, and 16 per cent is the retailer margin used in the scheduled ceiling price formula, not an escalation cap.`},{id:`q-pj-price-control-5`,topicId:`pj-price-control`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Four brands of a scheduled formulation qualify for the ceiling price calculation, with prices to retailer of Rs 20, Rs 24, Rs 26 and Rs 30 per strip. The ceiling price per strip, excluding tax, is closest to`,options:[`Rs 25.00`,`Rs 27.50`,`Rs 29.00`,`Rs 31.50`],answer:2,explanation:`The simple average price to retailer is (20 + 24 + 26 + 30) divided by 4, which is 100 divided by 4, that is Rs 25.00. Multiplying by 1.16 for the 16 per cent retailer margin gives Rs 29.00, so Rs 25.00 is the average before margin and is the classic trap answer.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pj-price-control-6`,topicId:`pj-price-control`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The list from which the First Schedule of DPCO 2013 draws the formulations placed under ceiling price control is the`,options:[`National List of Essential Medicines`,`Indian Pharmacopoeia index`,`Schedule H1 list`,`WHO model formulary`],answer:0,explanation:`The DPCO First Schedule is aligned with the National List of Essential Medicines, so a revision of NLEM leads to amendment of the Schedule and brings new molecules under control. Schedule H1 is a prescription control list under the D and C Rules and has no pricing effect.`},{id:`q-pj-price-control-7`,topicId:`pj-price-control`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In the cost-based formula R.P. = (M.C. + C.C. + P.M. + P.C.) x (1 + MAPE/100) + ED, the term MAPE stands for`,options:[`Minimum allowable production expense`,`Maximum allowable post-manufacturing expenses`,`Mean adjusted price escalation`,`Marginal allowance for packaging and excise`],answer:1,explanation:`MAPE is the maximum allowable post-manufacturing expenses, covering trade margin, promotion and distribution costs, and it was the mark-up applied over the cost build-up in the DPCO 1995 regime. It is not a production cost term, since M.C., C.C., P.M. and P.C. already carry the material, conversion and packing costs.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pj-price-control-8`,topicId:`pj-price-control`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which of the following is a function of NPPA rather than of CDSCO?`,options:[`Granting permission for a phase III clinical trial`,`Issuing an import licence in Form 10`,`Recovering amounts overcharged from consumers with interest`,`Approving a new fixed dose combination`],answer:2,explanation:`Recovery of overcharged amounts, with interest, is an enforcement function of NPPA under the DPCO. Clinical trial permission, import licensing and approval of new fixed dose combinations are all central drug regulatory functions exercised by CDSCO through the Drugs Controller General (India).`}],n=[{id:`f-pj-dc-act-1`,topicId:`pj-dc-act`,front:`D and C Act year and Rules year?`,back:`Act 1940, Rules 1945.`},{id:`f-pj-dc-act-2`,topicId:`pj-dc-act`,front:`Sections defining misbranded, adulterated and spurious drugs?`,back:`Misbranded 17, adulterated 17A, spurious 17B.`},{id:`f-pj-dc-act-3`,topicId:`pj-dc-act`,front:`Who heads DTAB and who is its Member-Secretary?`,back:`Chairman is the Director General of Health Services; Member-Secretary is the Drugs Controller General (India).`},{id:`f-pj-dc-act-4`,topicId:`pj-dc-act`,front:`Sections for Government Analysts and for Inspectors?`,back:`Government Analysts Section 20, Inspectors Section 21; powers 22, procedure 23.`},{id:`f-pj-dc-act-5`,topicId:`pj-dc-act`,front:`Purpose of the Drugs Consultative Committee?`,back:`To secure uniformity of administration of the Act throughout India.`},{id:`f-pj-dc-act-6`,topicId:`pj-dc-act`,front:`Forms for manufacturing licences?`,back:`Form 25 for other drugs (apply Form 24); Form 28 for Schedule C, C1 and X (apply Form 27).`},{id:`f-pj-schedules-1`,topicId:`pj-schedules`,front:`Schedule M, N, P, U - one line each?`,back:`M GMP and premises; N minimum equipment for a pharmacy; P life period and storage; U records of manufacture and raw materials.`},{id:`f-pj-schedules-2`,topicId:`pj-schedules`,front:`Label symbols for Schedule H, H1 and X?`,back:`H - red Rx; H1 - red Rx plus boxed warning with red border; X - red NRx.`},{id:`f-pj-schedules-3`,topicId:`pj-schedules`,front:`Record retention: Schedule H, H1 and X?`,back:`H two years, H1 separate register three years, X prescription in duplicate and records two years.`},{id:`f-pj-schedules-4`,topicId:`pj-schedules`,front:`Schedule C versus C1?`,back:`C - biological products such as sera, vaccines, toxins, antitoxins, insulin and hormones. C1 - other special products such as digitalis and ergot preparations.`},{id:`f-pj-schedules-5`,topicId:`pj-schedules`,front:`Schedule J deals with?`,back:`Diseases and ailments that a drug may not claim on its label to prevent or cure.`},{id:`f-pj-schedules-6`,topicId:`pj-schedules`,front:`Schedule Y covers what, and what largely superseded it?`,back:`Clinical trials and new drug import or manufacture; largely superseded by the New Drugs and Clinical Trials Rules, 2019.`},{id:`f-pj-pharmacy-act-1`,topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,front:`Section 42 of the Pharmacy Act says?`,back:`Only a registered pharmacist may compound, prepare, mix or dispense a medicine on a prescription; penalty up to six months imprisonment, or fine, or both.`},{id:`f-pj-pharmacy-act-2`,topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,front:`Who maintains the Central Register of Pharmacists?`,back:`The Pharmacy Council of India, compiled from the State registers.`},{id:`f-pj-pharmacy-act-3`,topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,front:`Ex-officio members of PCI?`,back:`Director General of Health Services, Drugs Controller General (India) and Director of the Central Drugs Laboratory, or their nominees.`},{id:`f-pj-pharmacy-act-4`,topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,front:`How many PCI members are nominated by the Central Government, and what is the condition?`,back:`Eight, of whom at least four must be registered pharmacists.`},{id:`f-pj-pharmacy-act-5`,topicId:`pj-pharmacy-act`,front:`Minimum qualification for registration as a pharmacist?`,back:`Diploma in Pharmacy from a PCI approved institution, including the prescribed practical training; a pharmacy degree also qualifies.`},{id:`f-pj-ndps-and-others-1`,topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,front:`NDPS punishment ladder by quantity?`,back:`Small - up to one year. Between small and commercial - up to ten years. Commercial - not less than ten, up to twenty years, with heavy fine.`},{id:`f-pj-ndps-and-others-2`,topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,front:`What does the NDPS definition of cannabis exclude?`,back:`The leaves and seeds of the plant when not accompanied by the flowering or fruiting tops.`},{id:`f-pj-ndps-and-others-3`,topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,front:`Four purposes for which drug advertisement is banned under Section 3 of the Magic Remedies Act?`,back:`Procurement of miscarriage or prevention of conception, improvement of capacity for sexual pleasure, correction of menstrual disorder, and any disease listed in the Schedule.`},{id:`f-pj-ndps-and-others-4`,topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,front:`Bonded versus non-bonded manufacture?`,back:`Bonded - duty-free alcohol used under bond and excise supervision. Non-bonded - duty-paid alcohol used, less supervision.`},{id:`f-pj-ndps-and-others-5`,topicId:`pj-ndps-and-others`,front:`What does the Poisons Act, 1919 empower Governments to do?`,back:`States may regulate possession for sale and sale of specified poisons; the Centre may regulate their import.`},{id:`f-pj-price-control-1`,topicId:`pj-price-control`,front:`DPCO is issued under which Act?`,back:`The Essential Commodities Act, 1955.`},{id:`f-pj-price-control-2`,topicId:`pj-price-control`,front:`Ceiling price formula under DPCO 2013?`,back:`Ceiling price = simple average price to retailer of brands with at least 1 per cent market share, multiplied by 1.16.`},{id:`f-pj-price-control-3`,topicId:`pj-price-control`,front:`Annual price increase allowed for a non-scheduled formulation?`,back:`Not more than 10 per cent over the preceding twelve months.`},{id:`f-pj-price-control-4`,topicId:`pj-price-control`,front:`Expand MAPE and give the cost-based retail price formula.`,back:`Maximum allowable post-manufacturing expenses. R.P. = (M.C. + C.C. + P.M. + P.C.) x (1 + MAPE/100) + ED.`},{id:`f-pj-price-control-5`,topicId:`pj-price-control`,front:`CDSCO is under which ministry, and who heads it?`,back:`Ministry of Health and Family Welfare through the Directorate General of Health Services; headed by the Drugs Controller General (India).`},{id:`f-pj-price-control-6`,topicId:`pj-price-control`,front:`How many medicines are in NLEM 2022?`,back:`384 medicines, grouped by therapeutic category and by level of healthcare.`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};